Subject: media: ipu6: ipu7: do not flag an ISYS reset for buffers left at stop

IPU7 firmware acks STREAM_FLUSH and STREAM_CLOSE without returning the
capture buffers it still holds: the last two or three queued buffers get
a FRAME_SOF but never a PIN_DATA_READY. return_buffers() then finds them
on the active list and sets need_reset, and video_open() fails with
-EIO ("isys power cycle required") until runtime suspend clears it.

The flag is also set after the stream has dropped its runtime PM
reference, so the suspend that should clear it can come first. On a
Surface Pro 12 (IPU 7.5) every stream stop left the flag set, and the
next open of any capture node failed until something else cycled the
ISYS runtime PM.

The staging ipu7 driver returned these buffers without a reset. Do the
same on IPU7: with the ISYS kept active by a running stream on another
port, five IR captures started and stopped back to back each delivered
every frame, and the other stream lost none.
---
diff --git a/drivers/media/pci/intel/ipu6/ipu6-isys-queue.c b/drivers/media/pci/intel/ipu6/ipu6-isys-queue.c
index 23d00e1f89e8..eebb0bc30e52 100644
--- a/drivers/media/pci/intel/ipu6/ipu6-isys-queue.c
+++ b/drivers/media/pci/intel/ipu6/ipu6-isys-queue.c
@@ -463,6 +463,14 @@ static void return_buffers(struct ipu6_isys_queue *aq,
 
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&aq->lock, flags);
 
+	/*
+	 * IPU7 firmware acks the flush and the close without returning
+	 * the buffers it still holds, so a non-empty active list is
+	 * expected there and the stream is already closed.
+	 */
+	if (IS_IPU7(av->isys->adev->isp))
+		need_reset = false;
+
 	if (need_reset) {
 		mutex_lock(&av->isys->mutex);
 		av->isys->need_reset = true;
